        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "The code is very similar (sans all the `IFDEF`s), but there are some differences.\r\n\r\nThis PR makes two core changes:\r\n1. Allows negative `row` and `col` values when the `MOUSE_FOCUS` flag is set. The `MOUSE_FOCUS` flag prevents `jump_to_mouse` from changing windows, it is set when dragging, in which case negative values are perfectly reasonable. Vim already does this: https://github.com/vim/vim/blob/1a08a3e2a584889f19b84a27672134649b73da58/src/mouse.c#L1730-L1733\r\n\r\n2. Unsets the `MOUSE_DID_MOVE` flag when dragging. The `MOUSE_DID_MOVE` flag is essentially an optimization, it turns `jump_to_flag` into a no-op when the mouse hasn't moved. But dragging can have side effects even when the mouse is stationary, so it's best to unset this. This change is only applicable to GUIs (I believe terminals only send mouse drag events when the mouse has moved). The various Vim GUIs have their own ways of handling this. For example, from MacVim: https://github.com/macvim-dev/macvim/blob/decde54d32f8640d63f7079e830c27552b7e1658/src/MacVim/MMTextViewHelper.m#L1035-L1050  \r\n\r\nSo I think there's a good case for making these changes in Neovim only.",
